There have been posts on this list [1], announcing that in the future
there will two physically different CPU versions. The NT/Linux version
will have some registers disabled, which, as far as I understood,
affects the top-end memory mapping capabilities of the CPU. Linux can
work around those limitations; NT doesn't care, 'cause it is 32-bit
anyway. Digital Unix won't run, and so won't VMS (although it also is
32-bit; don't ask, I don't understand it either).

Maurice, thanks for nice news about 433 and 500 MHz Alphas---it is a nice list
to read, thanks to all those scoops.

	Greetings
			przemek

[1] For example, on Wed, 21 Feb 96 11:13:47 MET, Dave Mosberger-Tang said:

  Neal := <crook@rdgeng.enet.dec.com>
  Neal> The two features that are (maybe) disabled in the -Pn
  Neal> versions are:
  Neal>     1) SPE[0] must always be SET in ICSR (Ibox csr)
  Neal>     2) SP[0] must always be SET in the MCSR (Mbox CSR).
  Neal>     These features control one of the superpage mappings. It
  Neal> is a static configuration that is performed during
  Neal> initialisation.
    
OK, that wouldn't be too bad.  It would mean that the top 2GB in the
virtual address space could not be mapped through the MMU because it
would identity map physical memory.  I presume this feature is used in
NT to deal efficiently with (kernel) 32-bit pointers that have bit 31
set---those pointers may get sign-extended to 64 bit.  If I recall
correctly, Jim's 32-bit port of Linux could have made use of this as
well.

